<Drawbacks/advantages are compared using "LOC" -> Load-Imbalance, Overlap, Communication>

= Infinite Reality (sort-middle)

= Sort-first -- Sort-last hybrid, Sort-first (papers)b

= Pixel Planes 5 (tile based rendering)
http://www.imgtec.com/powervr/insider/docs/POWERVR%20Series5%20Graphics.SGX%20architecture%20guide%20for%20developers.1.0.8.External.pdf

+ There is TBR and TBDR (Deferred Rendering). Think of it as a Modified Sort-Middle where after G, instead of passing primitives in 2D
space to the Rendered based on Screen Region, you instead write it to a buffer in memory (binning)

+ You are storing primitives in per Screen Region bins. 

+ Rasterizer picks from each bin where the Raster tile is guaranteed to be of a small size. Now pay attention ...
++ depth, color, stencil for such a small Screen space Tile is on chip. Now run the standard Raster pipeline.
   What you gain is that RMW on these buffers does not touch main memory hence a power and bandwidth win they argue

++ In this mode, in each bin, primitives are stored in OpenGL primitive submission order. Meaning depth test has to take place
   and you can still have overdraw.

Tile Based Deferred Rendering is the next step forwar where the primitives are stored in z-order thereby getting HSR. Sort of 
like a smarter depth pass. In addition, because the memory requirements are so low, extra memory is allocated on chip to store
geometry information like normal, color, some vertex information. After depth, shading and texture are applied. Hence no overdraw
and the name "Deferred Rendering". This is truly a win!!



= PixelFlow (Sort-Last)

= WireGL (sort-first, cluster based sw-rasterizer)
  < Q: I did not understand how they do the image
    composition. What about depth? In sort-first,
    each Image will be at full resolution and   
    the compositor will have to decide on depth
    analysis 
    A: One way is to retain depth information along
    with color and let the compositor do a final
    depth test. Keep in mind, individual rendering
    pipelines, will have done their own depth testing
    locally but a global depth testing needs to be 
    done to establish visibility across others.

    If we passed depth, then what about alpha? Shouldn't
    that be passed along too? Well, I guess in both
    sort-first and sort-last some information in addition
    to just the color needs to be shipped back to the
    compositor (the reducer)

    Update: Nope! You do not split primitives based on 
    Object space coordinates. You do so based on 2D
    Screen space coordinates. And yet this has to be done
    efficiently. You are taking 3D coordinates and finding
    their corresponding projections in 2D screen coordinate
    space
    http://www.cs.princeton.edu/~funk/sorting.pdf

    My approach would be to Sort-First and if the total #primitives
    is lower than a threshold, just pass it down the Geometry pipeline
    and use Clipping to get 2D boundary. Yes this would be like SFR SLI
    and compositing would need to be done on returned rectangular
    images.

    If total #primitives is higher than threshold, then find your own
    bounding 3D cube, say its cube #4 and find out all primitives that
    are contained within it. So here you would have to go from 2D tile
    to 3D boundary that matches it when reverse model-view-project
    transformed. That's it, after that finding primitives is a simple
    x,y,z test.
    >

=======
* Sort-First bins 3D primitives into 2D screen space using a quick algorithm.
  Then runs the entire pipeline for that bin. Later Image composition is 
  done without depth (because binning was in screen space)

* Sort-Last is very similar, it runs the entire pipeline and generates an 
  incomplete image (could be because of SLI-SFR nature or PixelFlow+deferred_shading
  but important thing is that Image Composition is the huge step here and
  this includes depth information)

Both of the above are very good from communication point of view and the pipelines
run w/o communication during a single frame.
They both have Load-Imbalance problems however. 
Overlap could be an issue with Sort-First

* Sort-Middle
 What you would do in a shared-memory interface. Very chatty, essentially Geometry could
 talk to all Rasterizers (aka mid-frame-rendering communication)

 Could have Load-Imbalance too.

